<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mérida, Yucatán</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mérida is the capital of the Mexican state of Yucatán. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-montejo-the-younger">Francisco de Montejo the Younger</a> founded it in 1542 on the site of the Maya city of T'hó, reusing stone from its pyramids. It was the seat of the Spanish province of Yucatán and of the breakaway Republic of Yucatán in the 1840s, and grew rich on henequen fibre in the late 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/merida-yucatan.jpg" alt="Mérida, Yucatán" width="320" height="533" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: the above mentioned · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Collage_Merida_Yucatan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mérida lies slightly inland from the north-west corner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yucatan-peninsula">Yucatán Peninsula</a>, about 35 kilometres from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a>. The land is very flat, only 9 metres above sea level, and almost no surface water exists, though several cenotes give access to underground water. Many Maya sites lie in the area, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichen-itza">Chichen Itza</a>, Uxmal, Oxkintok, Sayil and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabah">Kabah</a>. The historic centre follows the colonial Spanish pattern, with odd-numbered streets running east to west and even-numbered ones north to south, and with Calles 60 and 61 bounding the Plaza Grande.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Spanish conquistadors, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-montejo-the-younger">Francisco de Montejo the Younger</a> and Juan de la Cámara, founded Mérida in 1542 and named it after Mérida in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/extremadura">Extremadura</a>, whose Roman ruins the Maya ruins reminded them of. The city stands on the Maya city of Ti'ho, also called Ichkanzihóo, &quot;City of Five Hills&quot;, for its pyramids. Carved stones from the ruins went into the early Spanish buildings, and are visible in the cathedral, built in the late 16th century.</p><p>From colonial times to the middle of the 19th century Mérida was a walled city, intended to protect the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peninsulares">peninsulares</a> and criollos from periodic revolts by the Maya. Nahua troops who had accompanied Montejo settled in the districts of San Cristóbal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> and San Román and were exempt from taxes for their military help.</p><p>The city is called La Ciudad Blanca, the White City, perhaps for the white limestone of its colonial façades. A folk story says it was named because only white Europeans were allowed to live inside it, but the first arches at its entrances were commissioned only in 1690, almost 150 years after its foundation.</p><p>In the late 19th century the surrounding area prospered by growing henequen, whose fibre was made into rope and twine and also into a traditional drink. By the early 20th century factories made tobacco, molasses, rum, soap and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leather">leather goods</a>. Korean immigration to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> began in 1905, when more than a thousand people arrived in Yucatán from Jemulpo and settled around Mérida to work on henequen plantations.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Mérida is the capital and largest city of the Mexican state of Yucatán, with 921,770 people in 2020, and the cultural and financial centre of the peninsula. The Cathedral of Mérida is said to be the oldest cathedral on the mainland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. The city's heritage is a blend of Maya and Spanish culture formed in the colonial era.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mérida, Yucatán” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1542 Francisco de Montejo the Younger founded <b>Mérida</b> on the site of T'ho, using its stones for the new buildings, and Chakan became the core of the Province of Yucatán.</blockquote><a href="/state/chakan">Chakan</a></li><li><blockquote>It occupied a compact area south-east of present-day <b>Mérida</b>, between Ceh Pech, Ah Kin Chel and Sotuta.</blockquote><a href="/state/hocaba">Hocabá</a></li><li><blockquote>After two failed attempts, Francisco de Montejo the Younger conquered western Yucatán and founded <b>Mérida</b> in 1542 on the site of T'ho.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-yucatan">Province of Yucatán</a></li><li><blockquote>A federalist revolt led by Santiago Imán in 1839 to 1840 restored local autonomy, and in 1841 the peninsula declared itself independent, with <b>Mérida</b> as capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-yucatan">Republic of Yucatán</a></li></ul></section>
